Ladybird ignores a percentage min-width on a table cell, as a note in
css-tables-3 says, and still takes a length one into the cell’s outer
min-content width. Chrome 158 and WebKit 26.5 agree on all 9 cases in
the test (one cell, with a max-width, three cells, collapsed borders, a
width:100% table, a 50% min-width, a column’s percentage min-width, a
length min-width, and a percentage width), as Ladybird now does.
Firefox 151 differs: It takes a percentage min-width as the cell’s
percentage, so in a table of auto width the cell takes the rest of the
container.

Blink reads a cell’s min-width in InlineSizesFromStyle() only where it
IsFixed(), for CreateCellInlineConstraint(). WebKit’s AutoTableLayout::
recalcColumn() takes the cell’s own minLogicalWidthForColumnSizing() for
its column. And Gecko’s BasicTableLayoutStrategy raises a cell’s
percentage to a percentage min-isize where it ConvertsToPercentage(),
which is where Firefox differs.
